WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.187 --> 00:00:17.768
My plunge into the sea was so unexpected that I can't remember what I was thinking at the

2
00:00:17.788 --> 00:00:21.169
time. I'm a good swimmer, so I know I didn't panic.

3
00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:25.510
Once I was back at the surface, my first thought was to look for my ship.

4
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.711
Had the crew seen me fall?

5
00:00:28.471 --> 00:00:30.131
Had the Abraham Lincoln turned around?

6
00:00:30.851 --> 00:00:32.392
Did I have any hope of being saved?

7
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:37.836
I caught a glimpse of a ship disappearing in the east, its lights dying out in the distance.

8
00:00:38.757 --> 00:00:39.798
It was the Abraham Lincoln!

9
00:00:40.298 --> 00:00:40.959
I was doomed.

10
00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:43.241
"Help! Help!"

11
00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:44.081
I shouted.

12
00:00:45.002 --> 00:00:48.805
I swam desperately toward the ship, but my clothes made it difficult to move.

13
00:00:49.566 --> 00:00:51.848
I was sinking I was suffocating!

14
00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:57.413
"Help!" That was my last cry before I started to slip below the waves.

15
00:00:58.413 --> 00:01:03.629
Suddenly a strong hand seized my clothes, and I felt myself being pulled to the surface.

16
00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:10.332
Then I heard these words in my ear: "If you would kindly lean on my shoulder, you'll be

17
00:01:10.372 --> 00:01:11.452
able to swim more easily."

18
00:01:11.472 --> 00:01:16.874
"Conseil!" I cried as I seized my faithful servant's arm.

19
00:01:16.894 --> 00:01:18.515
"Is that really you?"

20
00:01:18.535 --> 00:01:22.957
"Yes, I'm here awaiting your orders," replied Conseil.

21
00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:26.078
"That horrible collision threw you into the sea too?"

22
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:30.320
"No, but being your servant, I naturally followed you into the water."

23
00:01:31.332 --> 00:01:32.433
"And what about the ship?"

24
00:01:32.873 --> 00:01:33.213
I asked.

25
00:01:34.154 --> 00:01:35.995
Conseil flipped over to float on his back.

26
00:01:36.015 --> 00:01:39.198
"I don't think you should count on that ship to rescue you.

27
00:01:39.518 --> 00:01:43.321
When I threw myself into the water, I heard the man at the wheel say that both the

28
00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:44.962
rudder and propeller were broken."

29
00:01:44.982 --> 00:01:49.325
"Broken?" "Yes, broken by the monster's teeth.

30
00:01:49.705 --> 00:01:54.969
That's the only damage to the Abraham Lincoln, but it's bad news for us because they can't steer

31
00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:55.669
the ship anymore."

32
00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:57.530
"Then we are lost!"

33
00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:58.411
I cried.

34
00:01:58.431 --> 00:02:01.128
"Perhaps," Conseil replied calmly.

35
00:02:01.148 --> 00:02:07.550
"But we can survive for at least several hours more, and one can do a lot in several

36
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:13.272
hours." Conseil's unruffled calmness encouraged me, and I swam more vigorously.

37
00:02:14.332 --> 00:02:19.413
Since the Abraham Lincoln couldn't turn back to rescue us, our only hope was that Captain Farragut had

38
00:02:19.494 --> 00:02:21.474
sent out the small boats to search for us.

39
00:02:22.514 --> 00:02:24.855
We vowed to try to wait for as long as possible.

40
00:02:25.835 --> 00:02:30.217
I decided that we should conserve our strength so that the two of us wouldn't be exhausted at

41
00:02:30.237 --> 00:02:30.917
the same time.

42
00:02:31.878 --> 00:02:38.162
Here's how we managed this: While one man floated on his back, the other man swam and pushed

43
00:02:38.222 --> 00:02:39.362
the floating man ahead of him.

44
00:02:40.443 --> 00:02:45.326
Taking turns for ten minutes at a time, we could swim for hours, perhaps until daybreak.

45
00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:50.228
The Abraham Lincoln's collision with the creature had taken place around eleven o'clock at night.

46
00:02:51.169 --> 00:02:55.751
I reckoned that we had eight hours to swim before sunrise, which we could do if we kept

47
00:02:55.791 --> 00:02:56.532
relieving each other.

48
00:02:57.952 --> 00:03:00.413
The sea was very calm, so that was in our favor.

49
00:03:01.293 --> 00:03:05.035
But around one o'clock in the morning, I was overcome with terrible fatigue.

50
00:03:05.915 --> 00:03:08.496
My arms and legs were seized with horrible cramps.

51
00:03:09.237 --> 00:03:13.178
Conseil had to keep me afloat and our survival depended on him alone.

52
00:03:14.179 --> 00:03:18.841
I heard the poor fellow panting and I felt that he couldn't keep up this effort much longer.

53
00:03:18.861 --> 00:03:20.281
"Leave me!"

54
00:03:20.762 --> 00:03:21.282
I said to him.

55
00:03:21.302 --> 00:03:23.143
"Leave my master?

56
00:03:23.343 --> 00:03:24.563
Never," he replied.

57
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:26.164
"I would rather drown."

58
00:03:27.168 --> 00:03:29.990
Just then the moon appeared at the edge of a thick cloud.

59
00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:34.273
The surface of the sea glittered with its rays, and we felt revived.

60
00:03:35.253 --> 00:03:37.955
I scanned the horizon and spotted the Abraham Lincoln!

61
00:03:38.856 --> 00:03:43.439
But there were no small boats on the water, and the ship appeared to be about five miles

62
00:03:43.499 --> 00:03:46.761
away. My swollen lips could make no sound.

63
00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:51.284
Conseil could still speak, and periodically I heard him cry out for help.

64
00:03:52.705 --> 00:03:54.506
We stopped moving for a moment and listened.

65
00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:56.027
"Did you hear that?"

66
00:03:56.448 --> 00:03:56.848
I murmured.

67
00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.101
"It sounded like someone answered you."

68
00:04:00.042 --> 00:04:00.962
"Yes! Yes!"

69
00:04:01.483 --> 00:04:04.525
said Conseil, and he gave one more despairing call.

70
00:04:05.565 --> 00:04:09.768
This time there was no mistake; a human voice had responded to Conseil's call.

71
00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:13.391
Was it another unfortunate victim who had fallen from the ship?

72
00:04:14.231 --> 00:04:17.073
Or was it a small boat hailing us in the darkness?

73
00:04:18.034 --> 00:04:23.357
Leaning on my shoulder, Conseil raised himself half out of the water and then fell back exhausted.

74
00:04:23.377 --> 00:04:24.698
"What did you see?"

75
00:04:25.239 --> 00:04:25.599
I asked.

76
00:04:25.619 --> 00:04:28.012
"I saw he whispered.

77
00:04:28.032 --> 00:04:31.074
"I saw But we shouldn't talk.

78
00:04:31.495 --> 00:04:32.575
We should save our strength."

79
00:04:33.596 --> 00:04:36.178
Conseil was once more propelling me through the water.

80
00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:42.122
Sometimes he raised his head and uttered a cry that was answered by a voice that came nearer

81
00:04:42.182 --> 00:04:42.702
and nearer.

82
00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:44.263
I barely heard it.

83
00:04:45.704 --> 00:04:49.467
My strength was exhausted; my mouth opened and filled with salt water.

84
00:04:50.447 --> 00:04:53.950
I raised my head for the last time, and then I sank below the surface.

85
00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.072
At this moment I struck a hard body.

86
00:04:57.884 --> 00:05:00.966
I clung to it, and then felt that I was being pulled up.

87
00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:02.387
I fainted.

88
00:05:03.187 --> 00:05:06.129
But I quickly came to because someone was trying to revive me.

89
00:05:06.149 --> 00:05:09.751
"Conseil," I murmured as I half opened my eyes.

90
00:05:09.771 --> 00:05:11.932
"Did you call me, Master?"

91
00:05:12.372 --> 00:05:13.073
asked Conseil.

92
00:05:13.953 --> 00:05:17.015
But it was not Conseil who was vigorously rubbing my arms and legs.

93
00:05:17.776 --> 00:05:18.716
It was Ned Land.

94
00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:20.897
"Ned!" I cried.

95
00:05:20.917 --> 00:05:23.959
"Were you thrown into the sea by the shock of the collision?"

96
00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:30.936
"Yes, but fortunately I was able to quickly climb onto this island, or, perhaps I should say, climb

97
00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:33.598
onto our narwhal," replied the harpooner.

98
00:05:33.618 --> 00:05:37.761
"I soon discovered why my harpoon couldn't penetrate its skin."

99
00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:39.742
"Why, Ned?"

100
00:05:40.203 --> 00:05:42.524
I said, thoroughly puzzled by this conversation.

101
00:05:42.544 --> 00:05:47.288
"Because, Professor, the beast is made of sheet metal."

102
00:05:48.329 --> 00:05:48.829
I sat up.

103
00:05:49.609 --> 00:05:52.852
The three of us were resting on top of something that was smooth and black.

104
00:05:53.736 --> 00:05:56.237
Most of it was submerged below the surface of the water.

105
00:05:57.238 --> 00:06:01.180
I kicked my heels against our creature and heard a metallic sound in return.

106
00:06:01.901 --> 00:06:03.241
There was no doubt about it.

107
00:06:04.122 --> 00:06:09.925
This monster, this creature, this animal that had puzzled the world was man-made.

108
00:06:10.906 --> 00:06:15.528
Ned was convinced that we were lying on the back of a submarine, and Conseil and I could

109
00:06:15.588 --> 00:06:16.368
only agree with him.

110
00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:22.412
Just then a bubbling began at the back of this strange thing, and the vessel began to move.

111
00:06:23.252 --> 00:06:24.373
We hung on for dear life.

112
00:06:24.393 --> 00:06:28.654
"As long as it sails horizontally, I don't mind," muttered Ned.

113
00:06:28.674 --> 00:06:32.036
"But if it decides to dive, we're in trouble."

114
00:06:33.596 --> 00:06:35.617
This long night continued to pass slowly.

115
00:06:36.337 --> 00:06:38.858
We clung to the strange vessel as it picked up speed.

116
00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:43.260
Several times I thought I heard some vague sounds coming from below us.

117
00:06:44.281 --> 00:06:48.462
It was almost as if someone had given a command and voices were responding.

118
00:06:49.323 --> 00:06:51.944
What kind of beings existed in this strange boat?

119
00:06:52.796 --> 00:06:53.216
I wondered.

120
00:06:53.897 --> 00:06:55.798
What was the source of its great speed?

121
00:06:56.719 --> 00:06:57.820
Daybreak finally appeared.

122
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.063
I was about to examine the hull upon which we rested when I felt the whole vessel starting

123
00:07:03.103 --> 00:07:03.524
to sink.

124
00:07:03.544 --> 00:07:07.987
"Oh!" cried Ned, furiously kicking the metal beneath his feet.

125
00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.628
"Open up, you inhospitable rascals!"

126
00:07:11.629 --> 00:07:13.190
The sinking movement stopped at once.

127
00:07:13.991 --> 00:07:15.632
We heard the sound of metal moving.

128
00:07:16.592 --> 00:07:19.254
An iron plate slid aside and a man popped up.

129
00:07:19.975 --> 00:07:22.897
He uttered a strange cry and immediately disappeared.

130
00:07:23.918 --> 00:07:30.345
Moments later eight masked men appeared and silently forced the three of us into their strange machine.
